t to  move the triangle  and now the triangle has become this  emergent object so instead of just  giving people these static images  instead of giving people just a bit  static images we want them to engage in  movement so that we can get that kind of  visual learning process that mapping a  physical phenomenon onto the visual  cortex especially at MIT right now Mary  throwing these ball you talk so if we  return to our question we have Mary  throwing these balls and there's this  tension especially at MIT right now  about how do I engage students in doing  this type of work I can have them do  math by hand we think that there is  definitely some value in that I can have  them solve it using a computer algebra  system or just coding it computer  programming or I can have them interact  with the widget and have them play with  this thing and learn visually and these  are three equivalently engaging ways to  learn for different people and we want  to know which one and how to give  different learners so that they engage  so that's kind of the premise of the  study that we were working on and it was  somewhat impossible to make this study  happen without some of the technology  that we had developed and one of the  reasons is because we did a 2x2  factorial design where each student in a  that took the course they were a part of  a specific group and this is the  breakdown of the groups they got either  an interactive widget or coding  instruction or not and the LMS systems  have a B testing and that kind of thing  but we wanted to make sure that every  group was equally separated so that  there wasn't all physics experts in the  first group in all physics experts in  the fourth group and then none of the  other ones knew anything about the topic  at hand and so we finally so using the  LTI system we were able to ask them  questions upfront grade their work and  place them into certain groups using a  clinical trial algorithm in real time  and so the results of our study came  through and what happened is we didn't  have enough participation in each of the  groups to come up with any results so I  know you're really helping me hear about  if these interactive visual widgets help  with learning but we don't know the  answer yet and but we did come up with  this text stack and I'm running out of  time so I I won't send that through but  the tech is what allowed us to be able  to even run the study in the first place  so what's next we've changed the premise  of our study we were looking at both  visual widgets encoding and how that  changes how students learn in the  classroom and we've changed our premise  so now just looking at the visual  widgets which allowed us to break it  down into two groups instead of five and  then we're also adding coding  instruction into the residential version  of this class because this is an online  class and so we get in 2020 we should be  able to then take that coding  instruction and incorporate it into this  study as a part of this study we also  ask students questions using a  manipulate so we gave them an empty I'm  going to be late and said move the  slider to a certain point that  represents the answer to this question  and we were able to use that the course  actually just ended like yesterday so we  were able to use that in our pre and  post-testing but I don't have that quite  yet built into the LTI package so that  should be coming soon we're also  creating computational labs for a  multivariable calculus class where we'll  be using that launch notebook thing that  grades the students so we haven't quite  used that yet but hopefully next year  we'll be able to talk about that and  then I also I'm just working on a phase  diagram Explorer for the material  science department so if any of you are  interested in that we can talk about  that later and that's all I have for  today  you  [Applause]
 

